r), but W8 pung is not honor, so impossible.\n\nWhat about **Half Flush** (6 points) + others? Half Flush requires only one suit and honors. My melds are Winds and W8 (Character suit). My hand has W1 (Character), T1/T3/T7 (Bamboo). So mixed suits\u2014not Half Flush.\n\nGiven the exposed melds, my best bet might be to aim for **All Pungs** (6 points) plus other patterns like **Pung of Terminals or Honors** (1 point each), **Prevalent Wind** (2 points if I get F4 pung), **Seat Wind** (2 points if I get F4 pung), **Dragon Pung** (2 points if I get dragon), etc., to reach 8.\n\nI have two pungs already. I need two more melds (pungs/kongs) and a pair. My hand has T1 triplet (can be pung), W1 pair (can be pair), T3 pair (can be pair or part of pung). So I'm close: I can form a pung from T1 (by keeping three T1), use W1 as pair, and need one more pung from T3 or other tiles. T3 pair needs one more T3 to pung. Unshown T3 count is 4, so possible. Alternatively, I could go for a kong with T1 (unshown T1 count is 4, I have 3, so need one more T1) but that would use all four, leaving me without a pair\u2014I'd need a pair elsewhere.\n\nPossible plan: Aim for All Pungs (6) + Pung of Terminals or Honors from T1 pung (1) + Pung of Terminals or Honors from F3 pung (1) = 8 points exactly. But F3 pung is an honor, so it gives 1 point. That totals 8. I also have W8 pung which gives no inherent points unless it becomes part of another pattern (e.g., if W8 becomes part of All Simples? But W8 is simple, but All Simples requires no terminals/honors, and I have terminals/honors, so not All Simples). So with T1 pung, F3 pung, W8 pung, and one more pung (e.g., T3 pung or other), and a pair (e.g., W1), I'd have All Pungs (4 pungs + pair). That yields 6 points. Plus Pung of Terminals or Honors for T1 pung (1) and F3 pung (1) = 8 points. That meets the threshold.\n\nBut wait, All Pungs requires four pungs/kongs. I have two exposed pungs (F3, W8). I need two more pungs. I have T1 triplet in hand (concealed pung if I keep it), and I need one more pung (e.g., T3 pung or other). So total four pungs. Pair from W1. That works.\n\nTo achieve that, I need to complete T1 as a pung (already have 3, so it's a concealed pung) and get a pung for T3 or something else. I have two T3, need one more T3. Unshown T3 count is 4, so good chance. Alternatively, I could pung W1 (I have two W1, need one more W1) but that would use my pair, so I'd need another pair. Better to keep W1 as pair and pung T3.\n\nThus, my immediate goal: discard tiles that don't help this plan. T7 is useless\u2014discard it. T1 I must keep all three. T3 I must keep both. W1 I must keep both.\n\nValid actions: Play T7, Play T1, Play W1, Play T3.  \n- Play T7: Discards a simple tile, keeping terminals and pairs. Safe and progresses plan.  \n- Play T1: Would break my T1 triplet, losing a pung\u2014bad.  \n- Play W1: Would break my potential pair\u2014bad.  \n- Play T3: Would reduce chance for T3 pung\u2014bad.\n\nSo only Play T7 makes sense.\n\nAlso, considering defense: No indication of opponents near winning, but discarding T7 is relatively safe\u2014unshown T7 count is 3, and it's a simple tile, less likely to be dangerous.\n\nEvaluation: The most feasible path to 8+ points is All Pungs combined with Pung of Terminals or Honors. This requires keeping T1 triplet, W1 pair, and pursuing T3 pung. Discarding T7 is optimal to advance this plan without harming necessary components.\n\nReason: Discarding T7 preserves all critical tiles for the planned hand, moves me closer to completing the required melds, and is a low-risk tile.\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
